failure_analysis.patterns_identified.reason:
Failure patterns are stated concretely by benchmark, topology and regime:
- square tie with naive on all benchmarks;
- non-trivial heavy-hex overhead in every case, largest for nonintegrable Ising;
- longer XY evolution not implying larger cost;
- XY hz=-0.7 heavy-hex depth regression (250 to 263) despite fewer CNOTs;
- width-one routing worse than naive on nonintegrable Ising (784 vs 670);
- rho=0.5 worsening integrable Ising (205 to 213, lower fidelity);
- persistent-vs-restoring reversal on XY;
- line Ising depth regression (177 to 185).

failure_analysis.patterns_explained_cohe.reason:
Each pattern is tied to a structural cause:
- square tie: zero routing increments when all bonds are native;
- depth regression: Eq. (5) prioritises SWAP count, depth is only a secondary key, and extra single-site rotations alter the critical path;
- width-one failure: greedy meeting-edge choice sacrifices future layouts;
- rho=0.5: a larger allowance unlocks cheap directions but forces extra later growth;
- largest Ising cost: expressibility saturation and routing structure rather than elapsed time.

